## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of advanced bit-twiddling for permutation of set bits. Evaluates ability to craft constant-time logic without loops and to reason about edge cases of the last permutation.

## Key Concepts

- Next higher permutation with fixed popcount
- Edge-case handling (max permutation -> 0)
- Pure bitwise operations and shifts; no loops or branches
- Efficient 64-bit handling and overflow behavior

## Code Example

```javascript
uint64_t nextBitPermutation64(uint64_t x) {
  uint64_t smallest = x & -x;
  uint64_t ripple = x + smallest;
  uint64_t ones = ripple & -ripple;
  uint64_t next = ripple | (((ones ^ ripple) >> 2) / smallest);
  return next;
}
```

## Examples

- `nextBitPermutation64(0b0011)` returns `0b0101` (next permutation with 2 set bits)
- `nextBitPermutation64(0b1100)` returns `0b10000` (next permutation with 2 set bits)
- `nextBitPermutation64(0b111000)` returns `0b1000011` (next permutation with 3 set bits)

## Why This Is Asked
This asks for deep bit-manipulation skill: a palindrome across 128 bits is a nontrivial symmetry that can be checked in constant time with a reversal network, not with loops. It reveals understanding of endian-ness and 128-bit layout.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit word as {uint64 hi, lo}
- Bit-reversal networks using masks: 0x5555..., 0x3333..., 0x0F0F..., etc.
- Palindrome property across all 128 bits
- Edge-case tests (all zeros, ends-set pattern)

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x){
  x = (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL | (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1;
  x = (x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL | (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2;
  x = (x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4;
  x = (x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8;
  x = (x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16;
  x = (x >> 32) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL | (x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) << 32;
  return x;
}
static inline uint128 reverse128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r; r.lo = reverse64(x.hi); r.hi = reverse64(x.lo); return r;
}
int isPalindromicBits128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r = reverse128(x);
  return (x.hi == r.hi) && (x.lo == r.lo);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to support streaming 128-bit palindromicity with partial data?
- What are the performance implications on 64-bit vs 128-bit targets?

## Why This Is Asked
Fresh bit-hack that demonstrates mastery of fixed-step SWAR technique and portability across languages.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR fixed-masks and staged swaps
- Bitwise rotation-free reversal
- Unsigned 32-bit wrap handling in JavaScript/TypeScript

## Code Example
```javascript
function reverseBits32(n) {
  n = ((n >>> 1) & 0x55555555) | ((n & 0x55555555) << 1);
  n = ((n >>> 2) & 0x33333333) | ((n & 0x33333333) << 2);
  n = ((n >>> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F) | ((n & 0x0F0F0F0F) << 4);
  n = ((n >>> 8) & 0x00FF00FF) | ((n & 0x00FF00FF) << 8);
  n = (n >>> 16) | (n << 16);
  return n >>> 0;
}
```

